    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-important title=\"Why isn't anything loading?\"}\n",
    "When you first load this, your broswer starts multiple R instances. I imagine there's a way to optimize that, but for now, wait a minute or two while the interactive parts load. If it doesn't load for you after waiting a few minutes, please shoot me an email so I can look into it. \n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook extends the midterm review with every test you are responsible for on the final. The CLT, z-test, one- and two-sample *t*-test, and one-way ANOVA sections are carried over from the midterm tool. The new material is confidence intervals and effect sizes (Section 5), post-hoc tests (Section 7), two-way ANOVA (Section 8), correlation (Section 9), linear regression (Section 10), and chi-square (Section 11).\n",
    "\n",
    "Playing with data is one of the best ways to build intuition for **what is going on inside** each test. When I first learned these, the formulas felt arbitrary until I could watch one slider move the whole picture. That is what this tool is for.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-tip title=\"If I were studying for the final...\"}\n",
    "Here is what I would do with this tool:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Drag the sliders until you can predict which way a statistic will move before it moves.\n",
    "- For each test, find the point where the result crosses the significance threshold, then say out loud why.\n",
    "- Practice naming your degrees of freedom every time you change a sample size.\n",
    "- After each visualization, cover the result and write the APA sentence yourself, then check it.\n",
    "- Spend the most time on Section 0. On the practice final, the single most common question is \"which test is appropriate, be as specific as possible.\"\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "# 0. Choosing the right test\n",
    "\n",
    "More points on this final come from picking the correct test than from any single calculation. The questions usually end with \"be as specific as possible,\" so a vague answer like \"a t-test\" or \"ANOVA\" will lose points. Name the full test, including between- vs within-subjects and one- vs two-way.\n",
    "\n",
    "Work down from your outcome variable.\n",
    "\n",
    "```{mermaid}\n",
    "flowchart TD\n",
    "    A[What is your outcome measure?] --> B[Counts / categories]\n",
    "    A --> C[A continuous score]\n",
    "\n",
    "    B --> B1[One categorical variable<br/>vs a hypothesized distribution]\n",
    "    B --> B2[Two categorical variables,<br/>testing for association]\n",
    "    B1 --> B1a[Chi-square goodness of fit]\n",
    "    B2 --> B2a[Chi-square test of independence]\n",
    "\n",
    "    C --> D[Relationship between<br/>two continuous variables?]\n",
    "    C --> E[Comparing means?]\n",
    "\n",
    "    D --> D1[Describe strength and direction]\n",
    "    D --> D2[Predict Y from X / fit a line]\n",
    "    D1 --> D1a[Pearson correlation r]\n",
    "    D2 --> D2a[Linear regression]\n",
    "\n",
    "    E --> F[One group vs a<br/>known/hypothesized value]\n",
    "    E --> G[Two conditions]\n",
    "    E --> H[Three or more conditions,<br/>one factor]\n",
    "    E --> I[Two factors]\n",
    "\n",
    "    F --> F1{Population SD known?}\n",
    "    F1 -->|Yes| F1a[z-test]\n",
    "    F1 -->|No| F1b[One-sample t-test]\n",
    "\n",
    "    G --> G1{Same subjects<br/>in both conditions?}\n",
    "    G1 -->|Different subjects| G1a[Independent-samples t-test]\n",
    "    G1 -->|Same subjects| G1b[Paired / related-samples t-test]\n",
    "\n",
    "    H --> H1{Same subjects<br/>across conditions?}\n",
    "    H1 -->|Different subjects| H1a[One-way between-subjects ANOVA]\n",
    "    H1 -->|Same subjects| H1b[One-way within-subjects ANOVA]\n",
    "\n",
    "    I --> I1[Two-way ANOVA<br/>two main effects + interaction]\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note title=\"Measurement scales (they show up every term)\"}\n",
    "The scale of your data decides what is even possible. Two mnemonics from the midterm review:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Order, Subtraction, Division, Zero**: \"Orange Snakes Dunk Zebras.\" Ordinal adds order, interval allows subtraction (equal spacing), ratio allows division and has a true zero.\n",
    "- **Nominal, Ordinal, Interval, Ratio**: \"No Order Is Right.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "Nominal and ordinal data are usually counts, which points you toward chi-square. Interval and ratio data are scores, which point you toward the mean-based tests and correlation/regression.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-important title=\"Null hypotheses, all in one place\"}\n",
    "| Test | Null hypothesis (H₀) |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| One-sample t / z | $\\mu = \\mu_0$ |\n",
    "| Independent-samples t | $\\mu_1 = \\mu_2$ |\n",
    "| Paired-samples t | $\\mu_d = 0$ |\n",
    "| One-way ANOVA | $\\mu_1 = \\mu_2 = \\dots = \\mu_k$ |\n",
    "| Two-way ANOVA | No main effect of A; no main effect of B; no A×B interaction |\n",
    "| Correlation | $\\rho = 0$ |\n",
    "| Linear regression | slope $\\beta = 0$ |\n",
    "| Chi-square | the variables are independent / observed frequencies match expected |\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-tip title=\"One-tailed or two-tailed?\"}\n",
    "The mean-based and relationship tests can be run one- or two-tailed: z-test, one-sample t, two-sample t, Pearson correlation, and linear regression. Chi-square is non-directional by construction, so it is not described as one- or two-tailed- you can think of it as functionally two-tailed if that helps.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# 1. Central Limit Theorem (CLT)\n",
    "\n",
    "The **Central Limit Theorem** states that the sampling distribution of the sample mean approaches a Normal distribution as the sample size (n) grows — regardless of the shape of the underlying population distribution.\n",
    "\n",
    "Key takeaways\n",
    "\n",
    "- The **mean** of the sampling distribution equals the population mean (μ)\n",
    "- The **spread** (standard error) shrinks as sample size *n* increases: SE = σ / √n\n",
    "- Even highly skewed or bimodal populations produce approximately Normal sampling distributions with enough samples\n",
    "\n",
    "The tendency for our samples to create this type of normal distribution is what enables most of our other statistical tests. \n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-important title=\"Why should I care about the Central Limit Theorem?\"}\n",
    "EVERY parametric test (i.e. everything except chi-square right now) depends on an assumption that there is a \"correct\" answer to your question, and that if you ask enough people, you will be able to guess or approximate the real answer. That's why we care about \"population\" metrics compared to \"sample\" metrics. We eventually need to assume that our observations are close enough to match the truth because we sampled enough people for each group of our test.  \n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "**How to use this app:** Choose a population shape, set the sample size (*n*), then drag the \"Number of samples\" slider slowly from left to right to watch the sampling distribution build up. Try setting your sample size to 1, 10, and 100. Do you see much of a difference beyond that?\n",
    "\n",
    "Notable milestones for a normally-distributed dataset: **1 sample** (almost nothing), **10 samples** (rough shape), **100 samples** (clearly bell-shaped), **300 samples** (well-converged).\n",
    "\n",
    "However, note how the sample size changes how quickly the correct mean is estimated. If we only take one measurement but include 100 people, we get pretty close to the correct answer. \n",
    "\n",
    "```{shinylive-r}\n",
    "#| viewerHeight: 550\n",
    "#| standalone: true\n",
    "\n",
    "library(shiny)\n",
    "\n",
    "ui <- fluidPage(\n",
    "  titlePanel(\"Central Limit Theorem\"),\n",
    "  sidebarLayout(\n",
    "    sidebarPanel(\n",
    "      selectInput(\"dist\", \"Population shape\",\n",
    "        choices = c(\"Normal\" = \"normal\",\n",
    "                    \"Right-skewed\" = \"skewed\",\n",
    "                    \"Uniform\" = \"uniform\",\n",
    "                    \"Bimodal\" = \"bimodal\")),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"n\", \"Sample size (n)\", min = 1, max = 50, value = 5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"n_sims\", \"Number of samples drawn\", min = 1, max = 300, value = 1),\n",
    "      hr(),\n",
    "      helpText(\"Drag 'Number of samples' slowly to watch the sampling distribution build up.\")\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    mainPanel(\n",
    "      plotOutput(\"cltPlot\", height = \"380px\"),\n",
    "      uiOutput(\"callout\")\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  )\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "server <- function(input, output) {\n",
    "\n",
    "  population <- reactive({\n",
    "    set.seed(110)\n",
    "    switch(input$dist,\n",
    "      normal  = rnorm(50, mean = 21, sd = 1.5),\n",
    "      skewed  = c(rnorm(40, mean = 19, sd = 1), rnorm(10, mean = 27, sd = 2)),\n",
    "      uniform = runif(200, min = 18, max = 26),\n",
    "      bimodal = c(rnorm(100, mean = 18, sd = 1), rnorm(100, mean = 24, sd = 1))\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  sample_means <- reactive({\n",
    "    set.seed(110)\n",
    "    pop <- population()\n",
    "    replicate(input$n_sims, mean(sample(pop, input$n, replace = TRUE)))\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$cltPlot <- renderPlot({\n",
    "    pop   <- population()\n",
    "    means <- sample_means()\n",
    "    n_sims <- input$n_sims\n",
    "    true_mean <- mean(pop)\n",
    "\n",
    "    par(mfrow = c(1, 2), mar = c(4, 4, 3, 1))\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Panel 1: Population\n",
    "    hist(pop,\n",
    "         breaks = 20,\n",
    "         col    = \"skyblue\",\n",
    "         border = \"white\",\n",
    "         main   = \"Population Distribution\",\n",
    "         xlab   = \"Value\",\n",
    "         xlim   = range(pop) + c(-1, 1))\n",
    "    abline(v = true_mean, col = \"red\", lwd = 3)\n",
    "    legend(\"topleft\", legend = paste(\"μ =\", round(true_mean, 2)),\n",
    "           col = \"red\", lwd = 2, bty = \"n\", cex = 0.85)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Panel 2: Sampling distribution\n",
    "    xlim_range <- range(c(means, pop)) + c(-1, 1)\n",
    "    hist(means,\n",
    "         breaks = max(5, min(30, n_sims %/% 5)),\n",
    "         col    = \"tomato\",\n",
    "         border = \"white\",\n",
    "         main   = sprintf(\"Sampling Distribution\\n(%d sample%s, n = %d)\",\n",
    "                          n_sims, if (n_sims == 1) \"\" else \"s\", input$n),\n",
    "         xlab   = \"Sample mean\",\n",
    "         xlim   = xlim_range)\n",
    "    abline(v = true_mean,   col = \"red\",  lwd = 3)\n",
    "    abline(v = mean(means), col = \"blue\", lwd = 3, lty = 2)\n",
    "    legend(\"topleft\",\n",
    "           legend = c(paste(\"True μ =\", round(true_mean, 2)),\n",
    "                      paste(\"x̄ of means =\", round(mean(means), 2))),\n",
    "           col = c(\"red\", \"blue\"), lwd = 2, lty = c(1, 2), bty = \"n\", cex = 0.85)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$callout <- renderUI({\n",
    "    means     <- sample_means()\n",
    "    true_mean <- mean(population())\n",
    "    n_sims    <- input$n_sims\n",
    "    se_theory <- sd(population()) / sqrt(input$n)\n",
    "    se_obs    <- sd(means)\n",
    "    milestone <- if (n_sims == 1) {\n",
    "      \"Just 1 sample — almost no distribution to see yet.\"\n",
    "    } else if (n_sims <= 10) {\n",
    "      paste0(\"After \", n_sims, \" samples: shape is very rough.\")\n",
    "    } else if (n_sims <= 100) {\n",
    "      paste0(\"After \", n_sims, \" samples: bell shape is emerging.\")\n",
    "    } else {\n",
    "      paste0(\"After \", n_sims, \" samples: well-converged to Normal.\")\n",
    "    }\n",
    "    tags$div(\n",
    "      style = \"background:#f0f4f8; border-left:4px solid #2c7bb6; padding:10px 14px; margin-top:10px; border-radius:4px;\",\n",
    "      tags$b(\"Snapshot: \"), milestone, tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"Running mean of sample means: %.3f (true μ = %.3f)\", mean(means), true_mean), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"Observed SE: %.3f | Theoretical SE (σ/√n): %.3f\", se_obs, se_theory)\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  })\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# 2. Z-Test\n",
    "\n",
    "A **z-test** is used when we know the **population standard deviation (σ)** — a rare but instructive scenario (e.g., when a large historical dataset gives us σ exactly).\n",
    "\n",
    "$$z = \\frac{\\bar{x} - \\mu_0}{\\sigma / \\sqrt{n}}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Because σ is known, we compare to the **Standard Normal** (not a *t*-distribution). The critical value is basically always ±1.96 for α = 0.05, two-tailed, regardless of *n*. This is directly in contrast to t-tests, where the critical values shrink as n incraeses. \n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-important title=\"The Null Hypothesis\"}\n",
    "The population mean equals our hypothesized value:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$H_0: \\mu = \\mu_0$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $\\mu_0$ is the specific value we're testing against (e.g., a national average).\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "**How to use this app:** Notice how the z-test and t-test give nearly identical results when *n* is large — the *t*-distribution approaches Normal.\n",
    "\n",
    "```{shinylive-r}\n",
    "#| viewerHeight: 480\n",
    "#| standalone: true \n",
    "\n",
    "library(shiny)\n",
    "\n",
    "ui <- fluidPage(\n",
    "  titlePanel(\"Z-Test (Known σ)\"),\n",
    "  sidebarLayout(\n",
    "    sidebarPanel(\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"mu0\",         \"Hypothesized mean (μ₀)\",  15, 25, 20, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"sample_mean\", \"Observed sample mean (x̄)\", 15, 25, 21, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"sigma\",       \"Known population SD (σ)\",  0.5, 8, 2,  step = 0.1),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"n\",           \"Sample size (n)\",           5, 200, 30)\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    mainPanel(\n",
    "      plotOutput(\"zPlot\", height = \"300px\"),\n",
    "      uiOutput(\"zResults\")\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  )\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "server <- function(input, output) {\n",
    "\n",
    "  z_stat <- reactive({\n",
    "    (input$sample_mean - input$mu0) / (input$sigma / sqrt(input$n))\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  p_val <- reactive({\n",
    "    2 * pnorm(-abs(z_stat()))\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$zPlot <- renderPlot({\n",
    "    z    <- z_stat()\n",
    "    z_c  <- 1.96\n",
    "    x    <- seq(-5, 5, length.out = 500)\n",
    "    y    <- dnorm(x)\n",
    "\n",
    "    par(mar = c(4, 4, 3, 1))\n",
    "    plot(x, y, type = \"l\", lwd = 2,\n",
    "         main = \"Standard Normal Distribution\",\n",
    "         xlab = \"z\", ylab = \"Density\")\n",
    "    x_lo <- x[x <= -z_c]; x_hi <- x[x >= z_c]\n",
    "    polygon(c(min(x_lo), x_lo, -z_c), c(0, dnorm(x_lo), 0),\n",
    "            col = rgb(0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.3), border = NA)\n",
    "    polygon(c(min(x_hi), x_hi, z_c), c(0, dnorm(x_hi), 0),\n",
    "            col = rgb(0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.3), border = NA)\n",
    "    abline(v =  z,   col = \"navy\",   lwd = 2.5, lty = 2)\n",
    "    abline(v =  z_c, col = \"tomato\", lwd = 1.5, lty = 3)\n",
    "    abline(v = -z_c, col = \"tomato\", lwd = 1.5, lty = 3)\n",
    "    legend(\"topleft\",\n",
    "           legend = c(sprintf(\"z = %.3f\", z),\n",
    "                      \"±z_crit = ±1.96\",\n",
    "                      \"Rejection region (α=0.05)\"),\n",
    "           col = c(\"navy\", \"tomato\", rgb(0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.5)),\n",
    "           lwd = c(2.5, 1.5, 8), lty = c(2, 3, 1), bty = \"n\", cex = 0.85)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$zResults <- renderUI({\n",
    "    z      <- z_stat()\n",
    "    p      <- p_val()\n",
    "    reject <- p < 0.05\n",
    "    color  <- if (reject) \"#c0392b\" else \"#27ae60\"\n",
    "    decision <- if (reject)\n",
    "      sprintf(\"Reject H₀: μ₀ = %.1f (p < 0.05)\", input$mu0)\n",
    "    else\n",
    "      sprintf(\"Fail to reject H₀: μ₀ = %.1f (p ≥ 0.05)\", input$mu0)\n",
    "    tags$div(\n",
    "      style = paste0(\"background:#f9f9f9; border-left:4px solid \", color,\n",
    "                     \"; padding:10px 14px; margin-top:10px; border-radius:4px;\"),\n",
    "      tags$b(\"Results:\"), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"z = %.3f\", z), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"p-value = %.4f\", p), tags$br(),\n",
    "      tags$b(decision)\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  })\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# 3. One-Sample *t*-Test\n",
    "\n",
    "A **one-sample *t*-test** asks: *Is our sample mean significantly different from a hypothesized population mean (μ₀)?* We often test against the hypothesis that our population mean is 0, and we test our sample after subtracting the sample mean from all observations. \n",
    "\n",
    "We use a *t*-statistic (not *z*) because **σ is unknown** and must be estimated from the sample:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$t = \\frac{\\bar{x} - \\mu_0}{s / \\sqrt{n}}, \\quad df = n - 1$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The *p*-value is the probability of observing a *t*-statistic this extreme (or more) if H₀ were true.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-important title=\"The Null Hypothesis\"}\n",
    "The population mean equals our hypothesized value:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$H_0: \\mu = \\mu_0$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The only difference from the z-test is that we estimate $\\sigma$ from our sample (as $s$) rather than knowing it.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "**How to use this app:** Adjust the sliders to see how moving μ₀ further from the observed mean, increasing *n*, or decreasing *s* all affect the *t*-statistic and *p*-value.\n",
    "\n",
    "```{shinylive-r}\n",
    "#| viewerHeight: 520\n",
    "#| standalone: true\n",
    "\n",
    "library(shiny)\n",
    "\n",
    "ui <- fluidPage(\n",
    "  titlePanel(\"One-Sample t-Test\"),\n",
    "  sidebarLayout(\n",
    "    sidebarPanel(\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"mu0\",         \"Hypothesized mean (μ₀)\",  15, 25, 20, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"sample_mean\", \"Observed sample mean (x̄)\", 15, 25, 21, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"sample_sd\",   \"Sample SD (s)\",            0.5, 5, 2,  step = 0.1),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"n\",           \"Sample size (n)\",           5, 100, 30)\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    mainPanel(\n",
    "      plotOutput(\"tPlot\", height = \"320px\"),\n",
    "      uiOutput(\"results\")\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  )\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "server <- function(input, output) {\n",
    "\n",
    "  stats <- reactive({\n",
    "    t_stat <- (input$sample_mean - input$mu0) / (input$sample_sd / sqrt(input$n))\n",
    "    df     <- input$n - 1\n",
    "    p_val  <- 2 * pt(-abs(t_stat), df)\n",
    "    list(t = t_stat, df = df, p = p_val)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$tPlot <- renderPlot({\n",
    "    s    <- stats()\n",
    "    df   <- s$df\n",
    "    t_ob <- s$t\n",
    "    t_crit <- qt(0.975, df)\n",
    "    x    <- seq(-5, 5, length.out = 500)\n",
    "    y    <- dt(x, df)\n",
    "\n",
    "    par(mar = c(4, 4, 3, 1))\n",
    "    plot(x, y, type = \"l\", lwd = 2,\n",
    "         main = sprintf(\"t-Distribution (df = %d)\", df),\n",
    "         xlab = \"t\", ylab = \"Density\")\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Shade rejection regions\n",
    "    x_lo <- x[x <= -t_crit]; x_hi <- x[x >= t_crit]\n",
    "    polygon(c(min(x_lo), x_lo, -t_crit), c(0, dt(x_lo, df), 0),\n",
    "            col = rgb(0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.3), border = NA)\n",
    "    polygon(c(min(x_hi), x_hi, t_crit), c(0, dt(x_hi, df), 0),\n",
    "            col = rgb(0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.3), border = NA)\n",
    "\n",
    "    abline(v = t_ob,    col = \"navy\",   lwd = 2.5, lty = 2)\n",
    "    abline(v =  t_crit, col = \"tomato\", lwd = 1.5, lty = 3)\n",
    "    abline(v = -t_crit, col = \"tomato\", lwd = 1.5, lty = 3)\n",
    "    legend(\"topleft\",\n",
    "           legend = c(sprintf(\"t = %.3f\", t_ob),\n",
    "                      sprintf(\"±t_crit = ±%.3f\", t_crit),\n",
    "                      \"Rejection region (α=0.05)\"),\n",
    "           col = c(\"navy\", \"tomato\", rgb(0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.5)),\n",
    "           lwd = c(2.5, 1.5, 8), lty = c(2, 3, 1), bty = \"n\", cex = 0.85)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$results <- renderUI({\n",
    "    s      <- stats()\n",
    "    reject <- s$p < 0.05\n",
    "    color  <- if (reject) \"#c0392b\" else \"#27ae60\"\n",
    "    decision <- if (reject)\n",
    "      sprintf(\"Reject H₀: μ₀ = %.1f (p < 0.05)\", input$mu0)\n",
    "    else\n",
    "      sprintf(\"Fail to reject H₀: μ₀ = %.1f (p ≥ 0.05)\", input$mu0)\n",
    "\n",
    "    tags$div(\n",
    "      style = paste0(\"background:#f9f9f9; border-left:4px solid \", color,\n",
    "                     \"; padding:10px 14px; margin-top:10px; border-radius:4px;\"),\n",
    "      tags$b(\"Results:\"), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"t(%d) = %.3f\", s$df, s$t), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"p-value = %.4f\", s$p), tags$br(),\n",
    "      tags$b(decision)\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  })\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# 4. Two-Sample *t*-Test\n",
    "\n",
    "A **two-sample *t*-test** tests whether the means of two groups differ:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$H_0: \\mu_1 = \\mu_2$$\n",
    "\n",
    "We can use either **independent** or **dependent** tests based on what our two groups represent. \n",
    "\n",
    "**When to use which:**\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Independent samples t-test:** Different subjects in each group (e.g., treatment vs. control with separate participants)\n",
    "- **Paired t-test:** Same subjects measured twice (e.g., pre-test vs. post-test, or same subject across two conditions)\n",
    "\n",
    "## 4a. Independent samples\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-important title=\"The Null Hypothesis\"}\n",
    "The two population means are equal:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$H_0: \\mu_1 = \\mu_2$$\n",
    "\n",
    "In other words, the difference between the means is zero: $\\mu_1 - \\mu_2 = 0$.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "Two variants:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Pooled (equal variances assumed):** combines variance estimates; uses *n₁ + n₂ − 2* df\n",
    "- **Welch (unequal variances):** uses separate variance estimates with adjusted (Satterthwaite) df — generally safer, but not something you need to know for this course!. It might be instructive for you to see the difference between the two though, especially when setting the variances far apart from one another. The degrees of freedom for this version are adjusted. \n",
    "\n",
    "**CRITICAL: DON'T WORRY ABOUT THE WELCH TEST FOR ANYTHING OTHER THAN CURIOSITY**\n",
    "(for now at least)\n",
    "\n",
    "**How to use this app:** Slide Group 1 and Group 2 means apart to see when the test becomes significant. Toggle between pooled and Welch to compare. \n",
    "\n",
    "```{shinylive-r}\n",
    "#| viewerHeight: 580\n",
    "#| standalone: true\n",
    "\n",
    "library(shiny)\n",
    "\n",
    "ui <- fluidPage(\n",
    "  titlePanel(\"Two-Sample t-Test\"),\n",
    "  sidebarLayout(\n",
    "    sidebarPanel(\n",
    "      tags$b(\"Group 1 (blue)\"),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"mu1\", \"Mean₁\",  10, 30, 20, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"sd1\", \"SD₁\",    0.5, 8,  2,  step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"n1\",  \"n₁\",     5,  80,  30),\n",
    "      tags$hr(),\n",
    "      tags$b(\"Group 2 (red)\"),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"mu2\", \"Mean₂\",  10, 30, 22, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"sd2\", \"SD₂\",    0.5, 8,  2,  step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"n2\",  \"n₂\",     5,  80,  30),\n",
    "      tags$hr(),\n",
    "      checkboxInput(\"equal_var\", \"Assume equal variances (pooled)\", value = TRUE)\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    mainPanel(\n",
    "      plotOutput(\"twoSamplePlot\", height = \"380px\"),\n",
    "      uiOutput(\"twoResults\")\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  )\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "server <- function(input, output) {\n",
    "\n",
    "  samples <- reactive({\n",
    "    set.seed(110)\n",
    "    g1 <- rnorm(input$n1, input$mu1, input$sd1)\n",
    "    g2 <- rnorm(input$n2, input$mu2, input$sd2)\n",
    "    list(g1 = g1, g2 = g2)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  test_result <- reactive({\n",
    "    samp <- samples()\n",
    "    t.test(samp$g1, samp$g2, var.equal = input$equal_var)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$twoSamplePlot <- renderPlot({\n",
    "    samp <- samples()\n",
    "    res  <- test_result()\n",
    "    t_ob <- res$statistic\n",
    "    df   <- res$parameter\n",
    "    t_crit <- qt(0.975, df)\n",
    "\n",
    "    par(mfrow = c(1, 2), mar = c(4, 4, 3, 1))\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Panel 1: overlapping histograms\n",
    "    all_vals <- c(samp$g1, samp$g2)\n",
    "    xlim_h   <- range(all_vals) + c(-1, 1)\n",
    "    hist(samp$g1, breaks = 15, col = rgb(0.18, 0.53, 0.78, 0.6),\n",
    "         border = \"white\", xlim = xlim_h,\n",
    "         main = \"Sample Distributions\", xlab = \"Value\")\n",
    "    hist(samp$g2, breaks = 15, col = rgb(0.82, 0.24, 0.24, 0.6),\n",
    "         border = \"white\", add = TRUE)\n",
    "    abline(v = mean(samp$g1), col = \"blue\", lwd = 2, lty = 2)\n",
    "    abline(v = mean(samp$g2), col = \"red\",  lwd = 2, lty = 2)\n",
    "    legend(\"topleft\",\n",
    "           legend = c(sprintf(\"G1: x̄=%.1f\", mean(samp$g1)),\n",
    "                      sprintf(\"G2: x̄=%.1f\", mean(samp$g2))),\n",
    "           fill = c(rgb(0.18, 0.53, 0.78, 0.6), rgb(0.82, 0.24, 0.24, 0.6)),\n",
    "           bty = \"n\", cex = 0.85)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Panel 2: t-distribution\n",
    "    x <- seq(max(-8, -abs(t_ob) - 3), max(8, abs(t_ob) + 3), length.out = 500)\n",
    "    y <- dt(x, df)\n",
    "    plot(x, y, type = \"l\", lwd = 2,\n",
    "         main = sprintf(\"t-Distribution (df = %.1f)\", df),\n",
    "         xlab = \"t\", ylab = \"Density\")\n",
    "    x_lo <- x[x <= -t_crit]; x_hi <- x[x >= t_crit]\n",
    "    if (length(x_lo) > 1)\n",
    "      polygon(c(min(x_lo), x_lo, -t_crit), c(0, dt(x_lo, df), 0),\n",
    "              col = rgb(0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.3), border = NA)\n",
    "    if (length(x_hi) > 1)\n",
    "      polygon(c(min(x_hi), x_hi, t_crit), c(0, dt(x_hi, df), 0),\n",
    "              col = rgb(0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.3), border = NA)\n",
    "    abline(v =  t_ob,   col = \"navy\",   lwd = 2.5, lty = 2)\n",
    "    abline(v =  t_crit, col = \"tomato\", lwd = 1.5, lty = 3)\n",
    "    abline(v = -t_crit, col = \"tomato\", lwd = 1.5, lty = 3)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$twoResults <- renderUI({\n",
    "    res    <- test_result()\n",
    "    t_ob   <- res$statistic\n",
    "    df     <- res$parameter\n",
    "    p_val  <- res$p.value\n",
    "    reject <- p_val < 0.05\n",
    "    color  <- if (reject) \"#c0392b\" else \"#27ae60\"\n",
    "    method <- if (input$equal_var) \"Pooled (equal variances)\" else \"Welch (unequal variances)\"\n",
    "    decision <- if (reject) \"Reject H₀: μ₁ = μ₂\" else \"Fail to reject H₀: μ₁ = μ₂\"\n",
    "    tags$div(\n",
    "      style = paste0(\"background:#f9f9f9; border-left:4px solid \", color,\n",
    "                     \"; padding:10px 14px; margin-top:10px; border-radius:4px;\"),\n",
    "      tags$b(\"Results (\", method, \"):\"), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"t(%.1f) = %.3f\", df, t_ob), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"p-value = %.4f\", p_val), tags$br(),\n",
    "      tags$b(decision)\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  })\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "## 4b. Paired Samples (Dependent t-Test)\n",
    "\n",
    "A **paired t-test** is used when the same subjects are measured under two conditions (e.g., before/after treatment, left hand vs right hand, condition A vs condition B for the same person). \n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-important title=\"The Null Hypothesis\"}\n",
    "There is no change in the mean when comparing the two observations:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$H_0: \\mu_d = 0$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $\\mu_d$ is the mean of the difference scores (Condition 2 − Condition 1).\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "Instead of comparing two independent groups, we compute the **difference score** for each subject and test whether the mean difference is significantly different from zero:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$t = \\frac{\\bar{d} - \\mu_0}{s_d / \\sqrt{n}}, \\quad df = n - 1$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $\\bar{d}$ is the mean of the difference scores, $s_d$ is the SD of the differences, and $n$ is the **number of pairs** (not total observations).\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note title=\"The Key Insight\"}\n",
    "By using the **same subjects** in both conditions, we can subtract out individual differences. This is analogous to the relationship between the **between-groups ANOVA** and **within-groups (repeated measures) ANOVA** in Section 6 — using the same subjects across conditions removes between-subject variability from the error term, dramatically increasing statistical power.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "**How to use this app:** Adjust the between-subject SD and within-subject (error) SD to see how the paired test gains power. When between-subject variability is high but within-subject error is low, the paired test dramatically outperforms the independent test on the same data. Notice how small of a difference can be detected if you maximize the sample size and minimize within-subject error! \n",
    "\n",
    "```{shinylive-r}\n",
    "#| viewerHeight: 620\n",
    "#| standalone: true\n",
    "\n",
    "library(shiny)\n",
    "\n",
    "ui <- fluidPage(\n",
    "  titlePanel(\"Paired t-Test\"),\n",
    "  sidebarLayout(\n",
    "    sidebarPanel(\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"n_pairs\", \"Number of subject pairs\", 5, 50, 20),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"mu_diff\", \"True mean difference (Condition 2 - Condition 1)\",\n",
    "                  -5, 5, 1.5, step = 0.25),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"sd_subj\", \"Between-subject SD\",\n",
    "                  0.5, 8, 3, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"sd_err\",  \"Within-subject error SD\",\n",
    "                  0.25, 5, 1, step = 0.25),\n",
    "      tags$hr(),\n",
    "      helpText(\"High between-subject SD + low error SD causes paired test to gain power vs. independent test.\")\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    mainPanel(\n",
    "      plotOutput(\"pairedPlot\", height = \"400px\"),\n",
    "      uiOutput(\"pairedResults\")\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  )\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "server <- function(input, output) {\n",
    "\n",
    "  paired_data <- reactive({\n",
    "    set.seed(110)\n",
    "    n <- input$n_pairs\n",
    "    subj_fx <- rnorm(n, 0, input$sd_subj)  # individual baseline\n",
    "    c1 <- subj_fx + rnorm(n, 0, input$sd_err)\n",
    "    c2 <- subj_fx + input$mu_diff + rnorm(n, 0, input$sd_err)\n",
    "    data.frame(subject = 1:n, c1 = c1, c2 = c2, diff = c2 - c1)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  paired_test <- reactive({\n",
    "    d <- paired_data()\n",
    "    t.test(d$c2, d$c1, paired = TRUE)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  indep_test <- reactive({\n",
    "    d <- paired_data()\n",
    "    t.test(d$c2, d$c1, paired = FALSE, var.equal = TRUE)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$pairedPlot <- renderPlot({\n",
    "    d <- paired_data()\n",
    "    res <- paired_test()\n",
    "    t_ob <- res$statistic\n",
    "    df   <- res$parameter\n",
    "    t_crit <- qt(0.975, df)\n",
    "\n",
    "    par(mfrow = c(1, 2), mar = c(4, 4, 3, 1))\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Panel 1: individual subject trajectories\n",
    "    ylim_r <- range(c(d$c1, d$c2)) + c(-1, 1)\n",
    "    plot(1:2, c(mean(d$c1), mean(d$c2)), type = \"n\",\n",
    "         xlim = c(0.5, 2.5), ylim = ylim_r,\n",
    "         xlab = \"Condition\", ylab = \"Value\",\n",
    "         main = \"Subject-level Differences\",\n",
    "         xaxt = \"n\")\n",
    "    axis(1, at = 1:2, labels = c(\"C1\", \"C2\"))\n",
    "\n",
    "    for (i in 1:nrow(d)) {\n",
    "      lines(1:2, c(d$c1[i], d$c2[i]), col = rgb(0, 0, 0.8, 0.3), lwd = 1)\n",
    "      points(1:2, c(d$c1[i], d$c2[i]), col = rgb(0, 0, 0.8, 0.4), pch = 16, cex = 0.8)\n",
    "    }\n",
    "    lines(1:2, c(mean(d$c1), mean(d$c2)), col = \"navy\", lwd = 3)\n",
    "    points(1:2, c(mean(d$c1), mean(d$c2)), col = \"navy\", pch = 19, cex = 1.6)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Panel 2: histogram of difference scores\n",
    "    hist(d$diff, breaks = 12, col = \"tomato\", border = \"white\",\n",
    "         main = \"Distribution of Differences\",\n",
    "         xlab = \"Difference (C2 - C1)\")\n",
    "    abline(v = 0, col = \"gray40\", lwd = 2, lty = 3)\n",
    "    abline(v = mean(d$diff), col = \"navy\", lwd = 3, lty = 2)\n",
    "    legend(\"topleft\",\n",
    "           legend = c(\"Zero (H₀)\", sprintf(\"Mean diff = %.2f\", mean(d$diff))),\n",
    "           col = c(\"gray40\", \"navy\"), lwd = 2, lty = c(3, 2), bty = \"n\", cex = 0.85)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$pairedResults <- renderUI({\n",
    "    res_p <- paired_test()\n",
    "    res_i <- indep_test()\n",
    "    t_p   <- res_p$statistic\n",
    "    p_p   <- res_p$p.value\n",
    "    t_i   <- res_i$statistic\n",
    "    p_i   <- res_i$p.value\n",
    "    reject <- p_p < 0.05\n",
    "    color  <- if (reject) \"#c0392b\" else \"#27ae60\"\n",
    "    decision <- if (reject) \"Reject H₀: Mean difference ≠ 0\"\n",
    "                else \"Fail to reject H₀: Mean difference = 0\"\n",
    "\n",
    "    tags$div(\n",
    "      style = paste0(\"background:#f9f9f9; border-left:4px solid \", color,\n",
    "                     \"; padding:10px 14px; margin-top:10px; border-radius:4px;\"),\n",
    "      tags$b(\"Paired t-Test Results:\"), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"t(%.0f) = %.3f\", res_p$parameter, t_p), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"p-value = %.4f\", p_p), tags$br(),\n",
    "      tags$b(decision), tags$br(), tags$br(),\n",
    "      tags$b(\"Compare to Independent Samples t-Test on same data:\"), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"t(%.0f) = %.3f, p = %.4f\", res_i$parameter, t_i, p_i), tags$br(),\n",
    "      tags$em(\"Notice how the paired test typically has a larger |t| and smaller p-value when between-subject variability is high.\")\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  })\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### Watch the error disappear\n",
    "\n",
    "The paired test's advantage is easiest to see by looking at the SAME dataset two ways. Every subject below has their own baseline; some people just score high and some score low. The left panel shows raw scores the way an independent test sees them: subject-to-subject spread swamps the condition effect. The right panel subtracts each subject's own average, which is exactly what working with difference scores accomplishes. The spread collapses, and the effect that was invisible on the left becomes unmistakable.\n",
    "\n",
    "```{shinylive-r}\n",
    "#| viewerHeight: 600\n",
    "#| standalone: true\n",
    "\n",
    "library(shiny)\n",
    "\n",
    "ui <- fluidPage(\n",
    "  titlePanel(\"Watch the Error Disappear\"),\n",
    "  sidebarLayout(\n",
    "    sidebarPanel(\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"n\",       \"Number of subjects\", 5, 30, 12),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"eff\",     \"True condition effect\", 0, 5, 2, step = 0.25),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"sd_subj\", \"Between-subject SD (baseline differences)\", 0, 10, 5, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"sd_err\",  \"Within-subject error SD\", 0.25, 3, 0.75, step = 0.25),\n",
    "      helpText(\"Same data, two views. The paired test works on the right panel.\")\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    mainPanel(\n",
    "      plotOutput(\"errPlot\", height = \"340px\"),\n",
    "      uiOutput(\"errResults\")\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  )\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "server <- function(input, output) {\n",
    "\n",
    "  dat <- reactive({\n",
    "    set.seed(110)\n",
    "    n <- input$n\n",
    "    base <- rnorm(n, 50, input$sd_subj)\n",
    "    c1 <- base + rnorm(n, 0, input$sd_err)\n",
    "    c2 <- base + input$eff + rnorm(n, 0, input$sd_err)\n",
    "    sm <- (c1 + c2) / 2\n",
    "    list(c1 = c1, c2 = c2, cen1 = c1 - sm, cen2 = c2 - sm, n = n)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  draw_panel <- function(y1, y2, main, sub) {\n",
    "    n <- length(y1)\n",
    "    ylim <- range(c(y1, y2)) + c(-1, 1)\n",
    "    plot(NA, xlim = c(0.6, 2.4), ylim = ylim, xaxt = \"n\",\n",
    "         xlab = \"Condition\", ylab = \"Score\", main = main)\n",
    "    axis(1, at = 1:2, labels = c(\"C1\", \"C2\"))\n",
    "    for (i in seq_len(n))\n",
    "      lines(1:2, c(y1[i], y2[i]), col = rgb(0, 0, 0.8, 0.25))\n",
    "    points(rep(1, n), y1, pch = 16, col = rgb(0.18, 0.53, 0.78, 0.6))\n",
    "    points(rep(2, n), y2, pch = 16, col = rgb(0.82, 0.24, 0.24, 0.6))\n",
    "    segments(0.85, mean(y1), 1.15, mean(y1), lwd = 4, col = \"navy\")\n",
    "    segments(1.85, mean(y2), 2.15, mean(y2), lwd = 4, col = \"red3\")\n",
    "    mtext(sub, side = 3, line = 0.2, cex = 0.8, col = \"gray40\")\n",
    "  }\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$errPlot <- renderPlot({\n",
    "    d <- dat()\n",
    "    par(mfrow = c(1, 2), mar = c(4, 4, 3.5, 1))\n",
    "    draw_panel(d$c1, d$c2, \"Raw scores\",\n",
    "               sprintf(\"independent view (overall SD = %.1f)\", sd(c(d$c1, d$c2))))\n",
    "    draw_panel(d$cen1, d$cen2, \"Subject-centered\",\n",
    "               sprintf(\"paired view (overall SD = %.1f)\", sd(c(d$cen1, d$cen2))))\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$errResults <- renderUI({\n",
    "    d <- dat()\n",
    "    ri <- t.test(d$c2, d$c1, var.equal = TRUE)\n",
    "    rp <- t.test(d$c2, d$c1, paired = TRUE)\n",
    "    tags$div(\n",
    "      style = \"background:#f9f9f9; border-left:4px solid #2c7bb6; padding:10px 14px; margin-top:10px; border-radius:4px;\",\n",
    "      tags$b(\"Same data, two tests:\"), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"Independent: t(%.0f) = %.2f, p = %.4f\", ri$parameter, ri$statistic, ri$p.value), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"Paired: t(%.0f) = %.2f, p = %.4f\", rp$parameter, rp$statistic, rp$p.value), tags$br(),\n",
    "      tags$em(\"The independent test must treat all subject-to-subject spread as error. The paired test subtracts it away before testing.\")\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  })\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# 5. Confidence Intervals & Effect Sizes\n",
    "\n",
    "Hypothesis testing gives you a yes/no decision. **Estimation** asks a different question: what range of values is plausible for the parameter? A **confidence interval** sets an upper and lower limit that, across repeated samples, contains the population value a set percentage of the time.\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\text{CI} = \\bar{x} \\pm t(s_{\\bar{x}}), \\quad s_{\\bar{x}} = \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note title=\"The link between a CI and a hypothesis test\"}\n",
    "A 95% confidence interval and a two-tailed test at α = 0.05 carry the same information. If the hypothesized value $\\mu_0$ falls **outside** the interval, you would reject H₀. If it falls **inside**, you retain H₀. Watch the decision box flip as you slide $\\mu_0$ across an interval edge.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "**Effect sizes** answer \"how big,\" which significance alone never tells you. The formula sheet gives you three:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Cohen's d** $= \\dfrac{\\bar{x} - \\mu_0}{s}$, in standard-deviation units (small 0.2, medium 0.5, large 0.8).\n",
    "- **Proportion of variance** $r^2 = \\dfrac{t^2}{t^2 + df}$ (item 11), the share of variability explained.\n",
    "- **Omega-squared** $\\omega^2 = \\dfrac{t^2 - 1}{t^2 + df}$ (item 12), a more conservative version of the same idea.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How to use this app:** Move $\\mu_0$ toward and past the interval edge and watch the decision flip. Then change $n$ and $s$ to see the interval tighten or widen, and notice that Cohen's d does **not** depend on $n$ while the p-value does.\n",
    "\n",
    "```{shinylive-r}\n",
    "#| viewerHeight: 560\n",
    "#| standalone: true\n",
    "\n",
    "library(shiny)\n",
    "\n",
    "ui <- fluidPage(\n",
    "  titlePanel(\"Confidence Interval & Effect Size (one mean)\"),\n",
    "  sidebarLayout(\n",
    "    sidebarPanel(\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"xbar\",  \"Observed sample mean (x̄)\", 15, 25, 21, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"mu0\",   \"Hypothesized mean (μ₀)\",   15, 25, 20, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"s\",     \"Sample SD (s)\",            0.5, 6, 2, step = 0.1),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"n\",     \"Sample size (n)\",          5, 100, 30),\n",
    "      selectInput(\"conf\",  \"Confidence level\",\n",
    "        choices = c(\"90%\" = 0.90, \"95%\" = 0.95, \"99%\" = 0.99), selected = 0.95)\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    mainPanel(\n",
    "      plotOutput(\"ciPlot\", height = \"330px\"),\n",
    "      uiOutput(\"ciResults\")\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  )\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "server <- function(input, output) {\n",
    "\n",
    "  stats <- reactive({\n",
    "    conf <- as.numeric(input$conf)\n",
    "    df   <- input$n - 1\n",
    "    se   <- input$s / sqrt(input$n)\n",
    "    tcrit <- qt(1 - (1 - conf) / 2, df)\n",
    "    lo   <- input$xbar - tcrit * se\n",
    "    hi   <- input$xbar + tcrit * se\n",
    "    t_stat <- (input$xbar - input$mu0) / se\n",
    "    p_val  <- 2 * pt(-abs(t_stat), df)\n",
    "    d      <- (input$xbar - input$mu0) / input$s\n",
    "    r2     <- t_stat^2 / (t_stat^2 + df)\n",
    "    w2     <- (t_stat^2 - 1) / (t_stat^2 + df)\n",
    "    list(conf = conf, df = df, se = se, lo = lo, hi = hi,\n",
    "         t = t_stat, p = p_val, d = d, r2 = r2, w2 = max(w2, 0))\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$ciPlot <- renderPlot({\n",
    "    s <- stats()\n",
    "    par(mfrow = c(1, 2), mar = c(4, 4, 3, 1))\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Panel 1: the interval on a number line\n",
    "    inside <- (input$mu0 >= s$lo && input$mu0 <= s$hi)\n",
    "    band   <- if (inside) rgb(0.15, 0.68, 0.38, 0.25) else rgb(0.75, 0.22, 0.17, 0.25)\n",
    "    xr <- range(c(s$lo, s$hi, input$mu0)) + c(-1, 1)\n",
    "    plot(NA, xlim = xr, ylim = c(0, 1), yaxt = \"n\", ylab = \"\",\n",
    "         xlab = \"Value\", main = sprintf(\"%d%% Confidence Interval\", round(s$conf * 100)))\n",
    "    rect(s$lo, 0.35, s$hi, 0.65, col = band, border = NA)\n",
    "    segments(s$lo, 0.5, s$hi, 0.5, lwd = 3, col = \"gray30\")\n",
    "    segments(c(s$lo, s$hi), 0.4, c(s$lo, s$hi), 0.6, lwd = 3, col = \"gray30\")\n",
    "    points(input$xbar, 0.5, pch = 19, cex = 1.8, col = \"navy\")\n",
    "    abline(v = input$mu0, col = \"tomato\", lwd = 2.5, lty = 2)\n",
    "    text(input$xbar, 0.78, sprintf(\"x̄ = %.2f\", input$xbar), col = \"navy\", cex = 0.9)\n",
    "    text(input$mu0, 0.22, sprintf(\"μ₀ = %.1f\", input$mu0), col = \"tomato\", cex = 0.9)\n",
    "    legend(\"topleft\", legend = sprintf(\"[%.2f, %.2f]\", s$lo, s$hi), bty = \"n\", cex = 0.9)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Panel 2: Cohen's d against conventional benchmarks\n",
    "    dd <- s$d\n",
    "    plot(NA, xlim = c(0, max(1, abs(dd) * 1.2)), ylim = c(0, 1), yaxt = \"n\", ylab = \"\",\n",
    "         xlab = \"|Cohen's d|\", main = \"Effect size (Cohen's d)\")\n",
    "    abline(v = c(0.2, 0.5, 0.8), col = \"gray70\", lty = 3)\n",
    "    text(c(0.2, 0.5, 0.8), 0.95, c(\"small\", \"medium\", \"large\"), col = \"gray50\", cex = 0.8)\n",
    "    segments(0, 0.5, abs(dd), 0.5, lwd = 8, col = rgb(0.18, 0.53, 0.78, 0.7))\n",
    "    points(abs(dd), 0.5, pch = 19, cex = 1.5, col = \"navy\")\n",
    "    text(abs(dd), 0.3, sprintf(\"d = %.2f\", dd), col = \"navy\", cex = 1)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$ciResults <- renderUI({\n",
    "    s <- stats()\n",
    "    inside <- (input$mu0 >= s$lo && input$mu0 <= s$hi)\n",
    "    color  <- if (inside) \"#27ae60\" else \"#c0392b\"\n",
    "    decision <- if (inside)\n",
    "      sprintf(\"μ₀ = %.1f is inside the interval: retain H₀ (consistent with p ≥ %.2f)\",\n",
    "              input$mu0, 1 - s$conf)\n",
    "    else\n",
    "      sprintf(\"μ₀ = %.1f is outside the interval: reject H₀ (consistent with p < %.2f)\",\n",
    "              input$mu0, 1 - s$conf)\n",
    "    tags$div(\n",
    "      style = paste0(\"background:#f9f9f9; border-left:4px solid \", color,\n",
    "                     \"; padding:10px 14px; margin-top:10px; border-radius:4px;\"),\n",
    "      tags$b(sprintf(\"%d%% CI: [%.2f, %.2f]\", round(s$conf * 100), s$lo, s$hi)), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"t(%d) = %.3f, p = %.4f\", s$df, s$t, s$p), tags$br(),\n",
    "      tags$b(decision), tags$br(), tags$br(),\n",
    "      tags$b(\"Effect sizes: \"),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"Cohen's d = %.3f | r² = %.3f | ω² = %.3f\", s$d, s$r2, s$w2), tags$br(),\n",
    "      tags$em(sprintf(\"r² says about %.0f%% of the variability is explained.\", 100 * s$r2))\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  })\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# 6. One-Way ANOVA\n",
    "\n",
    "ANOVA (Analysis of Variance) tests whether **the variance of 2 or more group means** are equal. In practice, it's usually used for **3 or more groups**, but it's important to understand that you can use it for comparing two groups.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note title=\"Why do we call it an analysis of VARIANCE if we care about the means?\"}\n",
    "Because the way you detect differences in means is by analyzing variance — specifically, by comparing two kinds of it.\n",
    "\n",
    "When group means differ, the data are spread out *between* groups more than you would expect by chance. ANOVA quantifies this by asking how much of the total variance is explained by group membership (between-groups variance) versus how much is just noise within each group (within-groups variance). The F-ratio is literally that comparison:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$F = \\frac{\\text{variance between groups}}{\\text{variance within groups}}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "If the group means are all equal, those two quantities should be about the same, so F ≈ 1. If the means differ, the between-groups variance inflates and F grows.\n",
    "\n",
    "So \"analysis of variance\" is accurate: the test never checks $\\mu_1 = \\mu_2 = \\mu_3$ directly. It infers a difference by decomposing and comparing variances. The means are what you care about; variance is the tool that gets you there.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Performing an ANOVA on only 2 groups\"}\n",
    "One-way ANOVA on two groups actually gives you the **same p-value as a pooled two-sample t-test!**\n",
    "\n",
    "In other words, when performing an ANOVA for two groups:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$F = t^2$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Remember that this is **NOT** the case when there are 3 or more groups, which is the usual time you'll see ANOVA being used.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-important title=\"The Null Hypothesis\"}\n",
    "There is no difference between any of the group means:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$H_0: \\mu_1 = \\mu_2 = \\mu_3 = \\dots = \\mu_k$$\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "If you get a significant *p*-value, the ANOVA only tells you that *something* differs. Finding *which* groups differ takes a post-hoc test, and the logic behind those matters enough to get its own section: see **Section 7** right below.\n",
    "\n",
    "It partitions total variance into:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Between-groups (SS_B):** variance due to group differences\n",
    "- **Within-groups / Error (SS_W):** variance within groups\n",
    "\n",
    "The **F-statistic** = MS_Between / MS_Within. Large F indicates that the **means differ more than chance**. Importantly, the test never compares the means directly: it infers that they differ by comparing variances.\n",
    "\n",
    "$$F = \\frac{MS_{Between}}{MS_{Within}} = \\frac{SS_B / (k-1)}{SS_W / (N-k)}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "## 6a. Between-Groups ANOVA\n",
    "\n",
    "Independent groups — different subjects in each condition. This is roughly analogous to the independent-sample *t*-test. You assume that there is no underlying structure or shared variance between your groups in this case. \n",
    "\n",
    "```{shinylive-r}\n",
    "#| viewerHeight: 620\n",
    "#| standalone: true\n",
    "\n",
    "library(shiny)\n",
    "\n",
    "ui <- fluidPage(\n",
    "  titlePanel(\"Between-Groups One-Way ANOVA\"),\n",
    "  sidebarLayout(\n",
    "    sidebarPanel(\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"k\", \"Number of groups\", 2, 5, 3),\n",
    "      tags$b(\"Group means (μ):\"),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"mu1g\", \"Group 1 mean\", 10, 30, 20, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"mu2g\", \"Group 2 mean\", 10, 30, 22, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"mu3g\", \"Group 3 mean\", 10, 30, 24, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      conditionalPanel(\"input.k >= 4\",\n",
    "        sliderInput(\"mu4g\", \"Group 4 mean\", 10, 30, 26, step = 0.5)),\n",
    "      conditionalPanel(\"input.k >= 5\",\n",
    "        sliderInput(\"mu5g\", \"Group 5 mean\", 10, 30, 28, step = 0.5)),\n",
    "      tags$hr(),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"sd_g\",  \"Within-group SD (all groups)\", 0.5, 8, 3, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"n_g\",   \"n per group\",                   5,  40, 20)\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    mainPanel(\n",
    "      plotOutput(\"anovaPlot\", height = \"350px\"),\n",
    "      tableOutput(\"anovaTable\"),\n",
    "      uiOutput(\"anovaResult\")\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  )\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "server <- function(input, output) {\n",
    "\n",
    "  anova_data <- reactive({\n",
    "    set.seed(110)\n",
    "    k   <- input$k\n",
    "    mus <- c(input$mu1g, input$mu2g, input$mu3g,\n",
    "             if (k >= 4) input$mu4g else NULL,\n",
    "             if (k >= 5) input$mu5g else NULL)[seq_len(k)]\n",
    "    grp_list <- lapply(seq_len(k), function(i)\n",
    "      data.frame(value = rnorm(input$n_g, mus[i], input$sd_g),\n",
    "                 group = factor(paste0(\"G\", i))))\n",
    "    do.call(rbind, grp_list)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  aov_result <- reactive({\n",
    "    d <- anova_data()\n",
    "    summary(aov(value ~ group, data = d))\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$anovaPlot <- renderPlot({\n",
    "    d   <- anova_data()\n",
    "    res <- aov_result()\n",
    "    F_ob   <- res[[1]]$`F value`[1]\n",
    "    df_bet <- res[[1]]$Df[1]\n",
    "    df_wit <- res[[1]]$Df[2]\n",
    "    F_crit <- qf(0.95, df_bet, df_wit)\n",
    "    pal    <- c(\"skyblue\", \"tomato\", \"seagreen\", \"gold\", \"orchid\")\n",
    "\n",
    "    par(mfrow = c(1, 2), mar = c(4, 4, 3, 1))\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Panel 1: boxplot of groups\n",
    "    boxplot(value ~ group, data = d,\n",
    "            col    = pal[seq_len(input$k)],\n",
    "            border = \"gray30\",\n",
    "            main   = \"Group Distributions\",\n",
    "            xlab   = \"Group\", ylab   = \"Value\")\n",
    "    stripchart(value ~ group, data = d,\n",
    "               method = \"jitter\", add = TRUE, pch = 16,\n",
    "               col = rgb(0, 0, 0, 0.3), vertical = TRUE)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Panel 2: F-distribution\n",
    "    x_max <- max(F_crit * 2.5, F_ob * 1.3, 6)\n",
    "    x <- seq(0, x_max, length.out = 500)\n",
    "    y <- df(x, df_bet, df_wit)\n",
    "    plot(x, y, type = \"l\", lwd = 2,\n",
    "         main = sprintf(\"F(%d, %d) Distribution\", df_bet, df_wit),\n",
    "         xlab = \"F\", ylab = \"Density\")\n",
    "    x_rej <- x[x >= F_crit]\n",
    "    if (length(x_rej) > 1)\n",
    "      polygon(c(F_crit, x_rej, max(x_rej)), c(0, df(x_rej, df_bet, df_wit), 0),\n",
    "              col = rgb(0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.3), border = NA)\n",
    "    abline(v = F_ob,   col = \"navy\",   lwd = 2.5, lty = 2)\n",
    "    abline(v = F_crit, col = \"tomato\", lwd = 1.5, lty = 3)\n",
    "    legend(\"topleft\",\n",
    "           legend = c(sprintf(\"F = %.3f\", F_ob),\n",
    "                      sprintf(\"F_crit = %.3f\", F_crit)),\n",
    "           col = c(\"navy\", \"tomato\"), lwd = 2, lty = c(2, 3), bty = \"n\", cex = 0.85)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$anovaTable <- renderTable({\n",
    "    res <- aov_result()\n",
    "    tbl <- res[[1]]\n",
    "    data.frame(\n",
    "      Source    = c(\"Between Groups\", \"Within Groups (Error)\"),\n",
    "      SS        = round(tbl$`Sum Sq`, 3),\n",
    "      df        = tbl$Df,\n",
    "      MS        = round(tbl$`Mean Sq`, 3),\n",
    "      F         = c(round(tbl$`F value`[1], 3), NA),\n",
    "      p         = c(round(tbl$`Pr(>F)`[1], 4), NA),\n",
    "      check.names = FALSE\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  }, na = \"\", striped = TRUE, bordered = TRUE, hover = TRUE)\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$anovaResult <- renderUI({\n",
    "    res    <- aov_result()\n",
    "    p_val  <- res[[1]]$`Pr(>F)`[1]\n",
    "    reject <- p_val < 0.05\n",
    "    color  <- if (reject) \"#c0392b\" else \"#27ae60\"\n",
    "    decision <- if (reject) \"Reject H₀: At least one group mean differs (p < 0.05)\"\n",
    "                else \"Fail to reject H₀: No significant difference between group means (p ≥ 0.05)\"\n",
    "    tags$div(\n",
    "      style = paste0(\"background:#f9f9f9; border-left:4px solid \", color,\n",
    "                     \"; padding:8px 14px; border-radius:4px;\"),\n",
    "      tags$b(decision)\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  })\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 6b. Within-Groups (Repeated Measures) ANOVA\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-important title=\"The Null Hypothesis\"}\n",
    "There is no change in the group mean across all observations:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$H_0: \\mu_1 = \\mu_2 = \\dots = \\mu_k$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where each $\\mu_i$ represents the mean for the same subjects measured under condition $i$.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "The same subjects are measured across all conditions. Subject-to-subject variability is **removed** from the error term, dramatically increasing statistical power. We do this by first calculating the mean difference WITHIN SUBJECT for each of the observations. This is how we had a data table that started out as 1000+ rows eventually turn into just 20 rows that we could perform our stats on in HW3; for each of the 4 conditions, we took the subject's average response, then compared those 4 averages per person. The \"Watch the error disappear\" demo back in Section 4b shows this same trick with two conditions; the repeated-measures ANOVA simply extends it to more.\n",
    "\n",
    "$$F_{RM} = \\frac{MS_{Conditions}}{MS_{Conditions \\times Subjects}}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The line plot below shows individual \"subjects\" across conditions. Parallel lines are good and indicate low within-subject error. This results in a high *F*.\n",
    "\n",
    "REMEMBER: While t-scores are universally accepted as having clearly delineated conventional effect thresholds (d = 0.2 | 0.5 | 0.8), **F tests do not have common effect sizes**. Depending on your dataset, explaining 25% of the variance could be excellent! However, if you're looking at a cancer screening test, it is likely far too low.  \n",
    "\n",
    "```{shinylive-r}\n",
    "#| viewerHeight: 620\n",
    "#| standalone: true\n",
    "\n",
    "library(shiny)\n",
    "\n",
    "ui <- fluidPage(\n",
    "  titlePanel(\"Within-Groups (Repeated Measures) ANOVA\"),\n",
    "  sidebarLayout(\n",
    "    sidebarPanel(\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"k_rm\",   \"Number of conditions\",    2, 4,   3),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"n_subj\", \"Number of subjects\",      5, 40,  20),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"mu_diff\",\"Mean difference across conditions\", 0, 8, 2, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"sd_subj\",\"Between-subject SD\",      0.5, 8, 3, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"sd_err\", \"Within-subject error SD\", 0.5, 5, 1, step = 0.25),\n",
    "      helpText(\"High between-subject SD + low error SD = RM ANOVA gains the most power.\")\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    mainPanel(\n",
    "      plotOutput(\"rmPlot\", height = \"380px\"),\n",
    "      tableOutput(\"rmTable\"),\n",
    "      uiOutput(\"rmResult\")\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  )\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "server <- function(input, output) {\n",
    "\n",
    "  rm_data <- reactive({\n",
    "    set.seed(110)\n",
    "    k       <- input$k_rm\n",
    "    n       <- input$n_subj\n",
    "    subj_fx <- rnorm(n, 0, input$sd_subj)  # random subject intercepts\n",
    "    cond_fx <- seq(0, input$mu_diff, length.out = k)\n",
    "\n",
    "    rows <- expand.grid(subject = 1:n, condition = 1:k)\n",
    "    rows$value <- subj_fx[rows$subject] + cond_fx[rows$condition] +\n",
    "                  rnorm(nrow(rows), 0, input$sd_err)\n",
    "    rows$subject   <- factor(rows$subject)\n",
    "    rows$condition <- factor(rows$condition,\n",
    "                             labels = paste0(\"C\", seq_len(k)))\n",
    "    rows\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  rm_aov <- reactive({\n",
    "    d <- rm_data()\n",
    "    summary(aov(value ~ condition + Error(subject/condition), data = d))\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$rmPlot <- renderPlot({\n",
    "    d   <- rm_data()\n",
    "    res <- rm_aov()\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Extract F from within-subject stratum\n",
    "    within_tbl <- res[[\"Error: subject:condition\"]][[1]]\n",
    "    F_ob   <- within_tbl$`F value`[1]\n",
    "    df_cond <- within_tbl$Df[1]\n",
    "    df_err  <- within_tbl$Df[2]\n",
    "    F_crit  <- qf(0.95, df_cond, df_err)\n",
    "\n",
    "    par(mfrow = c(1, 2), mar = c(4, 4, 3, 1))\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Panel 1: individual trajectories\n",
    "    conds   <- levels(d$condition)\n",
    "    n_conds <- length(conds)\n",
    "    cond_means <- tapply(d$value, d$condition, mean)\n",
    "    ylim_r  <- range(d$value) + c(-0.5, 0.5)\n",
    "\n",
    "    plot(1:n_conds, cond_means, type = \"n\",\n",
    "         xlim = c(0.5, n_conds + 0.5), ylim = ylim_r,\n",
    "         xlab = \"Condition\", ylab = \"Value\",\n",
    "         main = \"Individual Subject Trajectories\",\n",
    "         xaxt = \"n\")\n",
    "    axis(1, at = 1:n_conds, labels = conds)\n",
    "\n",
    "    for (subj in levels(d$subject)) {\n",
    "      vals <- d$value[d$subject == subj]\n",
    "      lines(1:n_conds, vals, col = rgb(0, 0, 0.8, 0.25), lwd = 1)\n",
    "    }\n",
    "    lines(1:n_conds, cond_means, col = \"navy\", lwd = 3)\n",
    "    points(1:n_conds, cond_means, col = \"navy\", pch = 19, cex = 1.4)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Panel 2: F distribution\n",
    "    x_max <- max(F_crit * 2.5, F_ob * 1.3, 6)\n",
    "    x <- seq(0, x_max, length.out = 500)\n",
    "    y <- df(x, df_cond, df_err)\n",
    "    plot(x, y, type = \"l\", lwd = 2,\n",
    "         main = sprintf(\"F(%d, %d) Distribution\", df_cond, df_err),\n",
    "         xlab = \"F\", ylab = \"Density\")\n",
    "    x_rej <- x[x >= F_crit]\n",
    "    if (length(x_rej) > 1)\n",
    "      polygon(c(F_crit, x_rej, max(x_rej)), c(0, df(x_rej, df_cond, df_err), 0),\n",
    "              col = rgb(0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.3), border = NA)\n",
    "    abline(v = F_ob,   col = \"navy\",   lwd = 2.5, lty = 2)\n",
    "    abline(v = F_crit, col = \"tomato\", lwd = 1.5, lty = 3)\n",
    "    legend(\"topleft\",\n",
    "           legend = c(sprintf(\"F = %.3f\", F_ob),\n",
    "                      sprintf(\"F_crit = %.3f\", F_crit)),\n",
    "           col = c(\"navy\", \"tomato\"), lwd = 2, lty = c(2, 3), bty = \"n\", cex = 0.85)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$rmTable <- renderTable({\n",
    "    res <- rm_aov()\n",
    "    within_tbl <- res[[\"Error: subject:condition\"]][[1]]\n",
    "    data.frame(\n",
    "      Source = c(\"Conditions (Between)\", \"Error (Conditions × Subjects)\"),\n",
    "      SS     = round(within_tbl$`Sum Sq`, 3),\n",
    "      df     = within_tbl$Df,\n",
    "      MS     = round(within_tbl$`Mean Sq`, 3),\n",
    "      F      = c(round(within_tbl$`F value`[1], 3), NA),\n",
    "      p      = c(round(within_tbl$`Pr(>F)`[1], 4), NA),\n",
    "      check.names = FALSE\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  }, na = \"\", striped = TRUE, bordered = TRUE, hover = TRUE)\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$rmResult <- renderUI({\n",
    "    res        <- rm_aov()\n",
    "    within_tbl <- res[[\"Error: subject:condition\"]][[1]]\n",
    "    p_val      <- within_tbl$`Pr(>F)`[1]\n",
    "    reject     <- p_val < 0.05\n",
    "    color      <- if (reject) \"#c0392b\" else \"#27ae60\"\n",
    "    decision   <- if (reject)\n",
    "      \"Reject H₀: Condition means differ significantly (p < 0.05)\"\n",
    "    else\n",
    "      \"Fail to reject H₀: No significant effect of condition (p ≥ 0.05)\"\n",
    "    tags$div(\n",
    "      style = paste0(\"background:#f9f9f9; border-left:4px solid \", color,\n",
    "                     \"; padding:8px 14px; border-radius:4px;\"),\n",
    "      tags$b(decision)\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  })\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# 7. Post-Hoc Tests & Multiple Comparisons\n",
    "\n",
    "A significant ANOVA tells you *that* the group means are not all equal. It does not tell you *which* groups differ. For that we follow up with a **post-hoc test**. In this class we used **Tukey's Honestly Significant Difference (HSD)**, which looks a lot like running a t-test on every pair of groups.\n",
    "\n",
    "The obvious question is why we need a special test instead of just running those t-tests ourselves. The answer comes down to making sure that you HONESTLY have a difference, and we do that by keeping track of how many total comparisons are being made. Every test you run carries its own chance of a false positive. If you do enough tests, eventually something is going to slip under that 5% line by accident. One test at p = 0.045 is fine. Ten tests, and your effective threshold should be MUCH lower, because each comparison brings its own chance of a fluke. With m comparisons, the chance of at least one false positive is approximately:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$P(\\text{at least one false positive}) \\approx 1 - (1 - \\alpha)^m$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Four groups already mean 6 pairwise comparisons, which pushes the family-wise error rate past 26%. Tukey's HSD accounts for the number of comparisons and holds the *overall* false-positive rate near 5%.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How to use this app:** Every group here is drawn from the SAME population, so any \"significant\" pairwise difference is a false positive by construction. Add groups and watch how often plain t-tests find something that is not there, while Tukey stays honest.\n",
    "\n",
    "```{shinylive-r}\n",
    "#| viewerHeight: 600\n",
    "#| standalone: true\n",
    "\n",
    "library(shiny)\n",
    "\n",
    "ui <- fluidPage(\n",
    "  titlePanel(\"Multiple Comparisons: Why Post-Hoc Corrections Exist\"),\n",
    "  sidebarLayout(\n",
    "    sidebarPanel(\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"k\",    \"Number of groups\", 3, 8, 4),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"n\",    \"n per group\",      5, 20, 10),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"nexp\", \"Simulated experiments\", 20, 200, 100, step = 20),\n",
    "      helpText(\"All groups come from the SAME population. Anything 'significant' is a false positive.\")\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    mainPanel(\n",
    "      plotOutput(\"phPlot\", height = \"330px\"),\n",
    "      uiOutput(\"phResults\")\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  )\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "server <- function(input, output) {\n",
    "\n",
    "  sim <- reactive({\n",
    "    set.seed(110)\n",
    "    k <- input$k; n <- input$n; B <- input$nexp\n",
    "    unc <- 0; tuk <- 0\n",
    "    for (b in seq_len(B)) {\n",
    "      g <- factor(rep(seq_len(k), each = n))\n",
    "      x <- rnorm(k * n, mean = 20, sd = 3)\n",
    "      pmat <- pairwise.t.test(x, g, p.adjust.method = \"none\")$p.value\n",
    "      if (min(pmat, na.rm = TRUE) < 0.05) unc <- unc + 1\n",
    "      padj <- TukeyHSD(aov(x ~ g))$g[, \"p adj\"]\n",
    "      if (min(padj) < 0.05) tuk <- tuk + 1\n",
    "    }\n",
    "    m <- choose(k, 2)\n",
    "    list(m = m, fwer = 1 - 0.95^m, unc = unc / B, tuk = tuk / B, B = B)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$phPlot <- renderPlot({\n",
    "    s <- sim()\n",
    "    par(mfrow = c(1, 2), mar = c(4, 4, 3, 1))\n",
    "\n",
    "    ms <- 1:28\n",
    "    plot(ms, 1 - 0.95^ms, type = \"l\", lwd = 2.5, col = \"navy\",\n",
    "         xlab = \"Number of comparisons (m)\",\n",
    "         ylab = \"P(at least one false positive)\",\n",
    "         main = \"False positives pile up\", ylim = c(0, 1))\n",
    "    abline(h = 0.05, col = \"gray50\", lty = 3)\n",
    "    points(s$m, s$fwer, pch = 19, col = \"tomato\", cex = 1.8)\n",
    "    text(s$m, min(s$fwer + 0.1, 0.97), sprintf(\"m = %d\", s$m), col = \"tomato\")\n",
    "\n",
    "    bp <- barplot(c(Uncorrected = s$unc, \"Tukey HSD\" = s$tuk),\n",
    "            col = c(\"tomato\", \"seagreen\"), border = \"white\",\n",
    "            ylim = c(0, max(0.5, s$unc + 0.12)),\n",
    "            main = sprintf(\"Fooled in %d null experiments\", s$B),\n",
    "            ylab = \"Proportion with a false positive\")\n",
    "    abline(h = 0.05, col = \"gray30\", lty = 2, lwd = 2)\n",
    "    text(bp, c(s$unc, s$tuk) + 0.04, sprintf(\"%.0f%%\", 100 * c(s$unc, s$tuk)))\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$phResults <- renderUI({\n",
    "    s <- sim()\n",
    "    tags$div(\n",
    "      style = \"background:#f9f9f9; border-left:4px solid #2c7bb6; padding:10px 14px; margin-top:10px; border-radius:4px;\",\n",
    "      sprintf(\"%d groups means %d pairwise comparisons.\", input$k, s$m), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"Theoretical chance of at least one fluke at α = .05: about %.0f%%.\", 100 * s$fwer), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"Simulated: plain t-tests got fooled in %.0f%% of experiments; Tukey HSD in %.0f%%.\",\n",
    "              100 * s$unc, 100 * s$tuk), tags$br(),\n",
    "      tags$em(\"Tukey gives up a little power in exchange for keeping the overall error rate near 5%.\")\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  })\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# 8. Two-Way ANOVA\n",
    "\n",
    "A **two-way between-subjects ANOVA** has two factors (call them A and B), with different subjects in every cell. Instead of one question it asks three, so it runs three hypothesis tests:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Main effect of A:** averaging over B, do the levels of A differ?\n",
    "- **Main effect of B:** averaging over A, do the levels of B differ?\n",
    "- **Interaction (A × B):** does the effect of A depend on the level of B?\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-important title=\"Reading the interaction plot\"}\n",
    "Plot the cell means with one line per level of B.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Parallel lines** mean no interaction. The gap between lines is the same at every level of A.\n",
    "- **Lines that converge, cross, or fan out** signal an interaction. The effect of one factor changes depending on the other.\n",
    "\n",
    "When the interaction is significant, interpret it **first**, using simple main effects, before reading the main effects on their own.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note title=\"Naming the parts (a common fill-in question)\"}\n",
    "For a design like \"type of media exposure (violent, non-violent, none) and age group (early vs late adolescent) on aggression scores\":\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Dependent variable:** aggression scores (the measured outcome).\n",
    "- **Factor A:** media exposure, with **3 levels**.\n",
    "- **Factor B:** age group, with **2 levels**.\n",
    "\n",
    "This is a 3 × 2 design. The app below uses a 2 × 2 so the interaction plot stays readable.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "**How to use this app:** Set the four cell means to make the two lines parallel and watch the interaction F drop toward nonsignificance. Then drag one corner to cross the lines and watch the interaction F climb.\n",
    "\n",
    "```{shinylive-r}\n",
    "#| viewerHeight: 640\n",
    "#| standalone: true\n",
    "\n",
    "library(shiny)\n",
    "\n",
    "ui <- fluidPage(\n",
    "  titlePanel(\"Two-Way Between-Subjects ANOVA (2 × 2)\"),\n",
    "  sidebarLayout(\n",
    "    sidebarPanel(\n",
    "      tags$b(\"Cell means\"),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"m11\", \"A1 / B1\", 10, 30, 18, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"m12\", \"A1 / B2\", 10, 30, 20, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"m21\", \"A2 / B1\", 10, 30, 22, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"m22\", \"A2 / B2\", 10, 30, 24, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      tags$hr(),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"sd_w\", \"Within-cell SD\", 0.5, 8, 3, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"n_c\",  \"n per cell\",      5, 40, 15)\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    mainPanel(\n",
    "      plotOutput(\"twoPlot\", height = \"320px\"),\n",
    "      tableOutput(\"twoTable\"),\n",
    "      uiOutput(\"twoResult\")\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  )\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "server <- function(input, output) {\n",
    "\n",
    "  dat <- reactive({\n",
    "    set.seed(110)\n",
    "    n <- input$n_c\n",
    "    mk <- function(mu) rnorm(n, mu, input$sd_w)\n",
    "    data.frame(\n",
    "      value = c(mk(input$m11), mk(input$m12), mk(input$m21), mk(input$m22)),\n",
    "      A = factor(rep(c(\"A1\", \"A1\", \"A2\", \"A2\"), each = n)),\n",
    "      B = factor(rep(c(\"B1\", \"B2\", \"B1\", \"B2\"), each = n))\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  fit <- reactive({\n",
    "    tab <- summary(aov(value ~ A * B, data = dat()))[[1]]\n",
    "    ss  <- tab[[\"Sum Sq\"]]\n",
    "    list(tab = tab, ss = ss, ss_tot = sum(ss))\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$twoPlot <- renderPlot({\n",
    "    d  <- dat()\n",
    "    cm <- tapply(d$value, list(d$A, d$B), mean)   # rows A1,A2 ; cols B1,B2\n",
    "    par(mar = c(4, 4, 3, 1))\n",
    "    yr <- range(cm) + c(-2, 2)\n",
    "    plot(NA, xlim = c(0.7, 2.3), ylim = yr, xaxt = \"n\",\n",
    "         xlab = \"Factor A\", ylab = \"Cell mean\",\n",
    "         main = \"Interaction plot (one line per level of B)\")\n",
    "    axis(1, at = 1:2, labels = c(\"A1\", \"A2\"))\n",
    "    lines(1:2, cm[, \"B1\"], col = \"navy\",   lwd = 3, type = \"b\", pch = 19, cex = 1.5)\n",
    "    lines(1:2, cm[, \"B2\"], col = \"tomato\", lwd = 3, type = \"b\", pch = 17, cex = 1.5)\n",
    "    legend(\"topleft\", legend = c(\"B1\", \"B2\"),\n",
    "           col = c(\"navy\", \"tomato\"), lwd = 3, pch = c(19, 17), bty = \"n\", cex = 0.9)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$twoTable <- renderTable({\n",
    "    f   <- fit()\n",
    "    tab <- f$tab\n",
    "    eta <- f$ss / f$ss_tot\n",
    "    data.frame(\n",
    "      Source = c(\"Factor A\", \"Factor B\", \"A × B\", \"Error\"),\n",
    "      SS     = round(f$ss, 2),\n",
    "      df     = tab[[\"Df\"]],\n",
    "      MS     = round(tab[[\"Mean Sq\"]], 2),\n",
    "      F      = c(round(tab[[\"F value\"]][1:3], 3), NA),\n",
    "      p      = c(round(tab[[\"Pr(>F)\"]][1:3], 4), NA),\n",
    "      \"eta sq\" = c(round(eta[1:3], 3), NA),\n",
    "      check.names = FALSE\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  }, na = \"\", striped = TRUE, bordered = TRUE, hover = TRUE)\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$twoResult <- renderUI({\n",
    "    tab <- fit()$tab\n",
    "    p   <- tab[[\"Pr(>F)\"]]\n",
    "    sig <- function(x) if (!is.na(x) && x < 0.05) \"significant\" else \"not significant\"\n",
    "    tags$div(\n",
    "      style = \"background:#f9f9f9; border-left:4px solid #2c7bb6; padding:10px 14px; border-radius:4px;\",\n",
    "      tags$b(\"Decisions (α = 0.05):\"), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"Main effect A: %s (p = %.4f)\", sig(p[1]), p[1]), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"Main effect B: %s (p = %.4f)\", sig(p[2]), p[2]), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"A × B interaction: %s (p = %.4f)\", sig(p[3]), p[3]), tags$br(),\n",
    "      tags$em(\"If the interaction is significant, interpret it before the main effects.\")\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  })\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# 9. Correlation (Pearson's r)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Correlation** describes the strength and direction of the linear relationship between two continuous variables. Pearson's *r* runs from −1 to +1: the sign is the direction, and the distance from 0 is the strength. A perfect correlation of 1 means every point sits exactly on a straight line: each 1 SD increase in X comes with exactly 1 SD increase in Y. Careful, though: r says nothing about the raw-unit slope. A perfect r can belong to a steep line or a shallow one; the slope is the regression line's job (Section 10).\n",
    "\n",
    "$$r = \\frac{SS_{XY}}{\\sqrt{SS_X \\, SS_Y}}, \\qquad r^2 = \\text{proportion of variance shared}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "On the exam you may be handed the sums of squares and asked for *r* and the effect size. With $SS_{XY}$ and the product $SS_X \\cdot SS_Y$ in hand, *r* is one division and a square root, and $r^2$ is just *r* squared (item 25, then the coefficient of determination).\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-important title=\"Significance depends on n, not just the size of r\"}\n",
    "A given *r* is significant only if it beats the **critical value** for your degrees of freedom ($df = n - 2$). With small samples even a fairly large *r* can fail to reach significance. For example, $r = .26$ with $n = 40$ does **not** clear the two-tailed .05 cutoff. Watch the critical value in the results box as you change *n*.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note title=\"What correlation cannot do\"}\n",
    "- **Causation.** A relationship does not tell you which variable drives which, or whether a third variable drives both.\n",
    "- **Outliers.** A single off-trend point can inflate or hide a correlation. Use the toggle below to see it.\n",
    "- **Non-linearity.** *r* only measures the straight-line part of a relationship. A strong curved pattern can still give *r* near 0.\n",
    "- **Restricted range** narrows the spread of scores and shrinks *r*.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "**How to use this app:** Set the underlying relationship strength and sample size, then read the observed *r*, its *r²*, and whether it clears the critical value. Flip on the outlier to see how much one point can move the result.\n",
    "\n",
    "```{shinylive-r}\n",
    "#| viewerHeight: 560\n",
    "#| standalone: true\n",
    "\n",
    "library(shiny)\n",
    "\n",
    "ui <- fluidPage(\n",
    "  titlePanel(\"Pearson Correlation\"),\n",
    "  sidebarLayout(\n",
    "    sidebarPanel(\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"rho\", \"Underlying relationship strength (population r)\",\n",
    "                  -0.95, 0.95, 0.5, step = 0.05),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"n\",   \"Sample size (n)\", 5, 150, 40),\n",
    "      checkboxInput(\"outlier\", \"Add one off-trend outlier\", value = FALSE),\n",
    "      helpText(\"df = n − 2. The critical value is the bar a correlation must clear to be significant.\")\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    mainPanel(\n",
    "      plotOutput(\"scatter\", height = \"330px\"),\n",
    "      uiOutput(\"corResults\")\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  )\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "server <- function(input, output) {\n",
    "\n",
    "  dat <- reactive({\n",
    "    set.seed(110)\n",
    "    n   <- input$n\n",
    "    rho <- input$rho\n",
    "    x <- rnorm(n)\n",
    "    y <- rho * x + sqrt(1 - rho^2) * rnorm(n)\n",
    "    x <- 50 + 10 * x\n",
    "    y <- 50 + 10 * y\n",
    "    if (input$outlier) {\n",
    "      x <- c(x, max(x) + 15)\n",
    "      y <- c(y, min(y) - 15)\n",
    "    }\n",
    "    data.frame(x = x, y = y)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  ct <- reactive({\n",
    "    d <- dat()\n",
    "    cor.test(d$x, d$y)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$scatter <- renderPlot({\n",
    "    d <- dat()\n",
    "    r <- unname(ct()$estimate)\n",
    "    par(mar = c(4, 4, 3, 1))\n",
    "    plot(d$x, d$y, pch = 19, col = rgb(0.18, 0.53, 0.78, 0.6),\n",
    "         xlab = \"X\", ylab = \"Y\",\n",
    "         main = sprintf(\"Scatter plot (r = %.3f)\", r))\n",
    "    fit <- lm(y ~ x, data = d)\n",
    "    abline(fit, col = \"navy\", lwd = 2)\n",
    "    if (input$outlier)\n",
    "      points(d$x[nrow(d)], d$y[nrow(d)], pch = 1, cex = 3, lwd = 2, col = \"tomato\")\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$corResults <- renderUI({\n",
    "    d   <- dat()\n",
    "    res <- ct()\n",
    "    r   <- unname(res$estimate)\n",
    "    df  <- unname(res$parameter)\n",
    "    p   <- res$p.value\n",
    "    tcrit <- qt(0.975, df)\n",
    "    rcrit <- tcrit / sqrt(df + tcrit^2)\n",
    "    reject <- p < 0.05\n",
    "    color  <- if (reject) \"#c0392b\" else \"#27ae60\"\n",
    "    decision <- if (reject)\n",
    "      sprintf(\"Significant: |r| = %.3f exceeds the critical r of %.3f\", abs(r), rcrit)\n",
    "    else\n",
    "      sprintf(\"Not significant: |r| = %.3f does not reach the critical r of %.3f\", abs(r), rcrit)\n",
    "    tags$div(\n",
    "      style = paste0(\"background:#f9f9f9; border-left:4px solid \", color,\n",
    "                     \"; padding:10px 14px; margin-top:10px; border-radius:4px;\"),\n",
    "      tags$b(sprintf(\"r = %.3f, r² = %.3f\", r, r^2)), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"t(%d) = %.3f, p = %.4f\", df, unname(res$statistic), p), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"Critical r (two-tailed, α = .05, df = %d): ±%.3f\", df, rcrit), tags$br(),\n",
    "      tags$b(decision), tags$br(),\n",
    "      tags$em(sprintf(\"r² means about %.0f%% of the variance in one variable is shared with the other.\",\n",
    "                      100 * r^2))\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  })\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# 10. Linear Regression\n",
    "\n",
    "Correlation describes a relationship; **linear regression** fits an equation to it so you can predict one variable from another.\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\hat{Y} = bX + a$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The **predictor** X is the known variable; the **criterion** Y is the one you predict. The slope *b* is the change in Y for a one-unit change in X (item 27). The intercept *a* is the predicted Y when X = 0. The line is chosen by **least squares**, the line that makes the squared vertical distances from the points (the **residuals**) as small as possible.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note title=\"Residuals and homoscedasticity\"}\n",
    "A **residual** is the gap between an observed point and the line: observed minus predicted. Plotting residuals against X is how you check the regression's assumptions.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Homoscedastic:** residuals show roughly even spread across X. This is what you want.\n",
    "- **Heteroscedastic:** the spread fans out or narrows across X. The line still fits, but the model's error is not constant, which violates an assumption.\n",
    "\n",
    "Use the toggle to switch between the two and watch the residual plot.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Prediction is just plugging in\"}\n",
    "To predict, substitute an X into the fitted equation. For $\\hat{Y} = -0.13X + 1.72$, a patient who attends 6 sessions has a predicted score of $-0.13(6) + 1.72 = 0.94$. You will not fit a regression by hand on this exam, but you are expected to read off the slope and intercept and predict from them.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "**How to use this app:** Set a slope and intercept, then move the prediction slider to read a predicted Y off the line. Turn on heteroscedasticity and watch the residual plot fan out while the line itself barely moves.\n",
    "\n",
    "```{shinylive-r}\n",
    "#| viewerHeight: 580\n",
    "#| standalone: true\n",
    "\n",
    "library(shiny)\n",
    "\n",
    "ui <- fluidPage(\n",
    "  titlePanel(\"Linear Regression\"),\n",
    "  sidebarLayout(\n",
    "    sidebarPanel(\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"b\",  \"Slope (b)\",            -3, 3, 1.2, step = 0.1),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"a\",  \"Intercept (a)\",        -5, 20, 5, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"noise\", \"Residual spread\",    0.5, 12, 4, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"n\",  \"Sample size (n)\",       10, 150, 50),\n",
    "      sliderInput(\"xpred\", \"Predict Y at X =\",   0, 20, 10, step = 0.5),\n",
    "      checkboxInput(\"hetero\", \"Make residuals heteroscedastic\", value = FALSE)\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    mainPanel(\n",
    "      plotOutput(\"regPlot\", height = \"340px\"),\n",
    "      uiOutput(\"regResults\")\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  )\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "server <- function(input, output) {\n",
    "\n",
    "  dat <- reactive({\n",
    "    set.seed(110)\n",
    "    n <- input$n\n",
    "    x <- runif(n, 0, 20)\n",
    "    sd_i <- if (input$hetero) input$noise * (0.25 + 1.5 * (x / 20)) else rep(input$noise, n)\n",
    "    y <- input$a + input$b * x + rnorm(n, 0, sd_i)\n",
    "    data.frame(x = x, y = y)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  fit <- reactive(lm(y ~ x, data = dat()))\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$regPlot <- renderPlot({\n",
    "    d   <- dat()\n",
    "    f   <- fit()\n",
    "    co  <- coef(f)\n",
    "    par(mfrow = c(1, 2), mar = c(4, 4, 3, 1))\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Panel 1: scatter + least-squares line + prediction\n",
    "    plot(d$x, d$y, pch = 19, col = rgb(0.18, 0.53, 0.78, 0.55),\n",
    "         xlab = \"X (predictor)\", ylab = \"Y (criterion)\",\n",
    "         main = \"Least-squares fit\")\n",
    "    abline(f, col = \"navy\", lwd = 2.5)\n",
    "    ypred <- co[1] + co[2] * input$xpred\n",
    "    segments(input$xpred, min(d$y) - 5, input$xpred, ypred, col = \"tomato\", lty = 2, lwd = 2)\n",
    "    segments(min(d$x) - 5, ypred, input$xpred, ypred, col = \"tomato\", lty = 2, lwd = 2)\n",
    "    points(input$xpred, ypred, pch = 19, col = \"tomato\", cex = 1.6)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Panel 2: residual plot\n",
    "    res <- resid(f)\n",
    "    plot(d$x, res, pch = 19, col = rgb(0.4, 0.4, 0.4, 0.6),\n",
    "         xlab = \"X\", ylab = \"Residual (observed − predicted)\",\n",
    "         main = \"Residual plot\")\n",
    "    abline(h = 0, col = \"navy\", lwd = 2, lty = 1)\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$regResults <- renderUI({\n",
    "    f  <- fit()\n",
    "    co <- coef(f)\n",
    "    r2 <- summary(f)$r.squared\n",
    "    ypred <- co[1] + co[2] * input$xpred\n",
    "    spread_note <- if (input$hetero)\n",
    "      \"Residuals fan out across X: heteroscedastic (assumption violated).\"\n",
    "    else\n",
    "      \"Residuals are evenly spread across X: homoscedastic (assumption met).\"\n",
    "    tags$div(\n",
    "      style = \"background:#f9f9f9; border-left:4px solid #2c7bb6; padding:10px 14px; margin-top:10px; border-radius:4px;\",\n",
    "      tags$b(sprintf(\"Fitted equation: Ŷ = %.3f·X + %.3f\", co[2], co[1])), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"r² = %.3f  (about %.0f%% of the variance in Y is explained by X)\", r2, 100 * r2), tags$br(),\n",
    "      sprintf(\"Predicted Y at X = %.1f:  Ŷ = %.2f\", input$xpred, ypred), tags$br(),\n",
    "      tags$em(spread_note)\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  })\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# 11. Chi-Square\n",
    "\n",
    "Chi-square is for **counts in categories**, not means. It compares the frequencies you observed against the frequencies you would expect. There are two versions, and the exam wants you to name which one.\n",
    "\n",
    "$$f_e = \\frac{\\text{row total} \\times \\text{column total}}{N}, \\qquad \\chi^2 = \\sum \\frac{(f_o - f_e)^2}{f_e}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Goodness of fit:** one categorical variable against a hypothesized distribution. $df = k - 1$, where *k* is the number of categories.\n",
    "- **Test of independence:** two categorical variables, testing whether they are associated. $df = (\\text{rows} - 1)(\\text{columns} - 1)$.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note title=\"The two chi-squares, side by side\"}\n",
    "Both flavors use the exact same statistic. What changes is the question, and where the expected frequencies come from.\n",
    "\n",
    "| | Goodness of fit | Test of independence |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Variables | one categorical variable | two categorical variables |\n",
    "| Question | does the observed distribution match a claimed one? | are the two variables associated? |\n",
    "| H₀ | observed frequencies match the expected distribution | the variables are independent |\n",
    "| Expected frequencies | from the hypothesized proportions (equal or not) | row total × column total / N |\n",
    "| df | k − 1 | (rows − 1)(columns − 1) |\n",
    "| Feels like | a one-sample test for categories | a correlation for categories |\n",
    "\n",
    "Two wording cues on the exam: \"matches / differs from an expected or claimed distribution\" points to goodness of fit, while \"related to / associated with / depends on\" points to independence. One more quirk worth remembering: goodness of fit is the rare test where *retaining* the null can be the interesting result, like confirming a die is fair.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-important title=\"Assumptions, including the soft one\"}\n",
    "- Each observation falls in **one and only one** category.\n",
    "- Observations are **independent** (different, unrelated participants). Splitting one person's count across two categories breaks this.\n",
    "- The **soft** assumption that researchers sometimes bend: every **expected** frequency should be at least **5**. The app warns you when this is violated.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note title=\"Effect size\"}\n",
    "For a 2 × 2 table, the **phi coefficient** $\\varphi = \\sqrt{\\chi^2 / N}$ (item 30). For larger tables, **Cramér's V** $= \\sqrt{\\chi^2 / (N \\cdot df_{smaller})}$ (item 31), where $df_{smaller}$ is the smaller of (rows − 1) and (columns − 1). For a 2 × 2 the two are identical.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "**How to use this app:** Switch between the two tests. In goodness-of-fit, push one category's count far from the others and watch χ² grow. In the independence test, make the rows proportional to each other (no association) and watch χ² fall toward 0.\n",
    "\n",
    "```{shinylive-r}\n",
    "#| viewerHeight: 620\n",
    "#| standalone: true\n",
    "\n",
    "library(shiny)\n",
    "\n",
    "ui <- fluidPage(\n",
    "  titlePanel(\"Chi-Square: Goodness of Fit and Independence\"),\n",
    "  sidebarLayout(\n",
    "    sidebarPanel(\n",
    "      selectInput(\"mode\", \"Which test?\",\n",
    "        choices = c(\"Goodness of fit\" = \"gof\", \"Test of independence\" = \"ind\")),\n",
    "      conditionalPanel(\"input.mode == 'gof'\",\n",
    "        tags$b(\"Observed counts (4 categories)\"),\n",
    "        sliderInput(\"o1\", \"Category 1\", 0, 60, 10),\n",
    "        sliderInput(\"o2\", \"Category 2\", 0, 60, 14),\n",
    "        sliderInput(\"o3\", \"Category 3\", 0, 60, 20),\n",
    "        sliderInput(\"o4\", \"Category 4\", 0, 60, 16),\n",
    "        helpText(\"Expected = equal split of the total across the 4 categories.\")\n",
    "      ),\n",
    "      conditionalPanel(\"input.mode == 'ind'\",\n",
    "        tags$b(\"Observed 2 × 2 table\"),\n",
    "        sliderInput(\"c11\", \"Row 1, Col 1\", 0, 100, 40),\n",
    "        sliderInput(\"c12\", \"Row 1, Col 2\", 0, 100, 20),\n",
    "        sliderInput(\"c21\", \"Row 2, Col 1\", 0, 100, 20),\n",
    "        sliderInput(\"c22\", \"Row 2, Col 2\", 0, 100, 40)\n",
    "      )\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    mainPanel(\n",
    "      plotOutput(\"chiPlot\", height = \"320px\"),\n",
    "      tableOutput(\"expTable\"),\n",
    "      uiOutput(\"chiResults\")\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  )\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "server <- function(input, output) {\n",
    "\n",
    "  analysis <- reactive({\n",
    "    if (input$mode == \"gof\") {\n",
    "      obs <- c(input$o1, input$o2, input$o3, input$o4)\n",
    "      test <- chisq.test(obs)\n",
    "      list(mode = \"gof\", obs = obs, exp = test$expected,\n",
    "           stat = unname(test$statistic), df = unname(test$parameter),\n",
    "           p = test$p.value, N = sum(obs))\n",
    "    } else {\n",
    "      m <- matrix(c(input$c11, input$c12, input$c21, input$c22),\n",
    "                  nrow = 2, byrow = TRUE)\n",
    "      test <- chisq.test(m, correct = FALSE)\n",
    "      list(mode = \"ind\", obs = m, exp = test$expected,\n",
    "           stat = unname(test$statistic), df = unname(test$parameter),\n",
    "           p = test$p.value, N = sum(m))\n",
    "    }\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$chiPlot <- renderPlot({\n",
    "    a <- analysis()\n",
    "    par(mar = c(4, 4, 3, 1))\n",
    "    if (a$mode == \"gof\") {\n",
    "      mat <- rbind(Observed = a$obs, Expected = a$exp)\n",
    "      barplot(mat, beside = TRUE, col = c(\"navy\", \"gray70\"), border = \"white\",\n",
    "              names.arg = paste0(\"C\", 1:4),\n",
    "              main = \"Observed vs Expected\", ylab = \"Frequency\")\n",
    "      legend(\"topright\", legend = c(\"Observed\", \"Expected\"),\n",
    "             fill = c(\"navy\", \"gray70\"), bty = \"n\")\n",
    "    } else {\n",
    "      barplot(a$obs, beside = TRUE, col = c(\"navy\", \"tomato\"), border = \"white\",\n",
    "              names.arg = c(\"Col 1\", \"Col 2\"),\n",
    "              main = \"Observed counts by row\", ylab = \"Frequency\")\n",
    "      legend(\"topright\", legend = c(\"Row 1\", \"Row 2\"),\n",
    "             fill = c(\"navy\", \"tomato\"), bty = \"n\")\n",
    "    }\n",
    "  })\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$expTable <- renderTable({\n",
    "    a <- analysis()\n",
    "    if (a$mode == \"gof\") {\n",
    "      data.frame(Category = paste0(\"C\", 1:4),\n",
    "                 Observed = a$obs, Expected = round(a$exp, 2),\n",
    "                 check.names = FALSE)\n",
    "    } else {\n",
    "      e <- round(a$exp, 2)\n",
    "      data.frame(Row = c(\"Row 1\", \"Row 2\"),\n",
    "                 \"Col 1 (exp)\" = e[, 1], \"Col 2 (exp)\" = e[, 2],\n",
    "                 check.names = FALSE)\n",
    "    }\n",
    "  }, striped = TRUE, bordered = TRUE)\n",
    "\n",
    "  output$chiResults <- renderUI({\n",
    "    a <- analysis()\n",
    "    reject <- a$p < 0.05\n",
    "    color  <- if (reject) \"#c0392b\" else \"#27ae60\"\n",
    "    rows <- nrow(as.matrix(a$exp)); cols <- ncol(as.matrix(a$exp))\n",
    "    df_small <- if (a$mode == \"ind\") min(rows - 1, cols - 1) else 1\n",
    "    eff <- sqrt(a$stat / (a$N * df_small))\n",
    "    eff_name <- if (a$mode == \"ind\" && rows == 2 && cols == 2) \"φ (phi)\" else\n",
    "                if (a$mode == \"ind\") \"Cramér's V\" else NA\n",
    "    decision <- if (reject) \"Reject H₀: the frequencies differ from expected / the variables are associated.\"\n",
    "                else \"Retain H₀: no significant departure from expected.\"\n",
    "    warn <- if (min(a$exp) < 5)\n",
    "      tags$div(style = \"color:#c0392b;\", \"Warning: an expected frequency is below 5 (assumption violated).\")\n",
    "    else NULL\n",
    "    tags$div(\n",
    "      style = paste0(\"background:#f9f9f9; border-left:4px solid \", color,\n",
    "                     \"; padding:10px 14px; margin-top:10px; border-radius:4px;\"),\n",
    "      tags$b(sprintf(\"χ²(%d) = %.3f, p = %.4f\", a$df, a$stat, a$p)), tags$br(),\n",
    "      if (a$mode == \"ind\") tags$span(sprintf(\"Effect size %s = %.3f\", eff_name, eff)) else\n",
    "        tags$span(sprintf(\"Total N = %d, k = 4 categories\", a$N)),\n",
    "      tags$br(),\n",
    "      tags$b(decision),\n",
    "      warn\n",
    "    )\n",
    "  })\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "NOTE: Please email me (benjamin.e.graul.gr@dartmouth.edu) if you find any errors in this tool!"
   ]
  }
